    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "The paper hosted on Academia.edu focuses on the 
iconography of the artifact known as the “Ring of Nestor” and situates it within
wider discussions of Aegean Bronze Age forgeries and authenticity. It treats the
Ring of Nestor as a gold signet ring traditionally attributed to the Aegean 
Bronze Age and closely examines the imagery engraved on its bezel. The study 
analyzes the figures and scenes depicted on the ring, explores interpretations 
such as ritual or afterlife imagery, and compares the ring’s iconography with 
other known Minoan and Mycenaean sealstones and signet rings. A significant part
of the paper discusses debates over whether the Ring of Nestor is an authentic 
Bronze Age artifact or a modern forgery, referencing the history of its 
discovery, subsequent reproductions, and its reception in scholarship. The 
author uses stylistic, iconographic, and technical criteria, as well as the 
ring’s alleged provenance, to assess its authenticity and to place it within the
broader phenomenon of Aegean forgeries.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The Journal of Hellenic Studies article titled “The 
Ring of Nestor”: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and A Sepulchral Treasure
of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia presents a detailed 
study of a gold signet ring known as the Ring of Nestor and of a group of 
associated gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê in Boeotia. The author 
provides a physical and stylistic description of the Ring of Nestor, treating it
as an Aegean Bronze Age gold signet ring linked to Minoan or Mycenaean culture. 
The article describes the ring’s iconography in detail and interprets the scenes
as offering a glimpse into the Minoan afterlife or after-world. It situates the 
ring within a sepulchral treasure reportedly found at or associated with Thisbê,
emphasizing the funerary context of the assemblage. The study discusses the 
provenance of the treasure, tracing its origin to Thisbê, and explains how the 
rings and seals came to scholarly attention, while also comparing the Ring of 
Nestor to other Aegean gold signet rings in terms of style and cultural 
significance.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The JSTOR article “A Signet-Ring in the City Art 
Museum of St. Louis” examines a specific signet ring held in the City Art Museum
of St. Louis and situates it within the broader typology of Aegean and related 
signet rings. The author describes the St. Louis ring’s material, engraving, and
stylistic features, and compares the depth of its engraving to that of Mycenaean
gold rings and Ionic examples. In doing so, the article explicitly references 
the Ring of Nestor, citing it as an example of a Mycenaean gold signet ring and 
referencing its illustration in Evans’s work (Palace of Minos, volume IV, figure
915b). The comparison uses the Ring of Nestor as a benchmark for engraving 
depth, stylistic traits, and chronological placement, reinforcing its status in 
scholarship as a canonical Mycenaean gold signet ring. The article thus provides
contextual information about the Ring of Nestor’s type and stylistic 
characteristics while focusing primarily on the St. Louis ring.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Metropolitan Museum of Art essay “Historic Images
of the Greek Bronze Age” discusses how key Greek Bronze Age artifacts became 
known and visualized in the late 19th and early 20th centuries through 
photographs, drawings, casts, and reproductions. It focuses on well-known 
objects such as the Phaistos Disk and gold signet rings identified as the Ring 
of Minos and the Ring of Nestor. The essay highlights the role of artists and 
copyists, particularly Gilliéron, in producing and disseminating images and 
replicas of these artifacts, and it examines how such reproductions shaped 
scholarly and public perceptions. It notes that some of these celebrated 
objects, including the signet rings, later became entangled in debates over 
authenticity and forgery. In this context, the Ring of Nestor is presented as a 
famous gold signet ring of the Aegean Bronze Age whose image circulated widely 
and whose status was reconsidered as questions about possible forgeries arose.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The blog post “The Lord of the Gold Rings. The 
Griffin Warrior of Pylos” discusses the discovery and significance of the 
Griffin Warrior grave near Pylos and focuses on the gold signet rings found in 
that burial. It describes the specific gold signet ring discovered by Blegen’s 
team at Pylos and analyzes its form, decoration, and cultural implications. In 
the course of comparing the Pylos ring to other famous Aegean rings, the author 
mentions the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, noting similarities in the 
construction of the ring bands. The post observes that the band of the Pylos 
ring is strikingly similar to the bands of the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of 
Minos, implying a shared type or construction technique. The overall aim is to 
connect the Griffin Warrior’s rings with broader traditions of Mycenaean and 
Minoan gold signet rings, although the blog represents a non-peer-reviewed 
personal interpretation.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d6",
      "document_summary": "The Academia.edu paper “ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN 
SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE” examines a notable Mycenaean 
gold signet ring (CMS I, 179) from the Tiryns treasure. It provides detailed 
information on the ring’s discovery, stating that it was unearthed in the lower 
town of Tiryns in December 1915 by the archaeologist Apostolos Arvanitopoulos. 
The article classifies the ring as a type IV golden ring, characterized as a 
thick reed-hammered roll, and discusses the form of the band and the engraved 
bezel. It situates this ring within the typology of Mycenaean signet rings and 
compares it with other famous Aegean gold rings. In this comparative context, 
the study refers to well-known rings such as the Ring of Nestor and uses them to
illustrate formal and stylistic parallels, thereby providing indirect contextual
information relevant to the classification and typology of the Ring of Nestor.",
      "source_ref": "s6"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d7",
      "document_summary": "The 1977 issue of the Nestor Bibliography of Aegean 
and Related Areas is an annotated bibliographic compilation listing scholarly 
publications on Aegean archaeology and related subjects. Among its entries, it 
includes a reference to a work titled “The Ring of Nestor”: A glimpse into the 
Minoan after-world, and a spectacular treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia. This entry confirms the title and subject of 
the article on the Ring of Nestor and connects it explicitly with a treasure of 
gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê in Boeotia. The bibliography 
provides a standardized citation for this work and situates it within the wider 
literature on Aegean Bronze Age signet rings and treasures.",
      "source_ref": "s7"
    }
