   Nestor”的图像学进行分析，重点是与爱琴海地区伪造品及真伪争论相关的内容。 [1]  
 • 文章集中讨论被称作“Ring of Nestor”的金质印章戒指，将其与一批来自博奥蒂亚     
   Thisbe                                                                       
   的金质印章戒指和珠状印玺一起分析，并将其场景解读为与“米诺人的来世世界”相关。 
   [2]                                                                          
 • 该文讨论来自博奥蒂亚 Thisbê 的一批金质印章戒指及珠状印玺，其中重点对象即“Ring
   of                                                                           
   Nestor”。文章详细描述戒指实物特征（材质、尺寸、工艺）、出土墓葬的情况、与其他
   金质印章戒指的关系，以及其图像场景在米诺与迈锡尼宗教、葬俗史上的意义。 [3]   
 • 章节标题为“Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from      
   Thisbê, Boeotia; 43-75 II. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world; Tafel I -  
   V”，包括对“Ring of                                                           
   Nestor”所在墓葬的葬具、“金质印章戒指和珠状印玺宝藏”的描述、相关图版，以及对戒
   指图像场景的解读。 [4]                                                       
 • 该文题为“The Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian                      
   Iconography”，研究重点在于戒指的“伪米诺”性质与埃及图像学元素，讨论了米诺和迈 
   锡尼金戒制作工艺并提出一类“金戒指类型学”。 [5]                               
 • 该研究展示了“Ring of Nestor”黄金含量达到                                     
   97%，说明其高品质古代爱琴海工艺水平，并与其他重要作品比较。 [6]              
 • PDF 文档包括关于“Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult”和“The Ring of               
   Nestor”等内容，并提到多个“Gold ring in … National Museum”等条目，将“Ring of  
   Nestor”用作迈锡尼宗教图像例证。 [7]                                          
 • 文章讨论了某枚来自迈锡尼的金戒上的羽饰或羽状装饰：“What appear to be breasts 
   on the sphinxes of the gold ring from Mycenae (m) may                        
   …”，并以此重新解读戒指图像中的身体或装饰细节。 [8]                           
 • 该研究针对四座圆顶墓中出土的黄金物件进行技术分析，以推进对迈锡尼黄金生产的认 
   识，并在参考文献中列出“The ring of Nestor. A glimpse into the Minoan         
   after-world. London …”作为与迈锡尼金器工艺相关的经典研究。 [9]               
 • Evans’s study of the sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals 
   from the tomb at Thisbê, Boeotia, identifies and names one gold signet ring  
   from this assemblage as the “Ring of Nestor” and uses it as a key example for
   interpreting Aegean religious symbolism and funerary traditions. [3][4]      
 • Evans interprets the iconography of the Ring of Nestor as depicting a scene  
   related to the Minoan afterlife, situating the ring within a broader         
   discussion of Aegean religious symbolism. [4]                                
 • Childe’s article in Nature focuses on the Ring of Nestor within the Thisbê   
   gold signet-ring and bead-seal treasure and interprets its image as          
   representing a scene associated with the Minoan afterworld and funerary      
   customs. [2]                                                                 
 • Modern scholarship has developed a sustained debate over the authenticity of 
   the Ring of Nestor, examining its status as a possible pseudo‑Minoan or      
   forged object through metallurgical analysis, iconographic study, and        
   stylistic comparison. [1][5][6]                                              
 • Vlachopoulos reports that the gold composition of the Ring of Nestor is      
   approximately 97% pure, and uses this high fineness to argue for its         
   high-quality workmanship within Aegean goldworking traditions. [6]           
 • One strand of modern research, exemplified by the study titled “The          
   Pseudo‑Minoan Nestor Ring and Its Egyptian Iconography,” argues that the Ring
   of Nestor’s imagery incorporates or imitates Egyptian religious and funerary 
   motifs and evaluates whether the ring should be classified as a pseudo‑Minoan
   creation on the basis of iconographic and typological comparison of Minoan   
   and Mycenaean gold rings. [5]                                                
 • At least one dedicated iconographic study of the Ring of Nestor analyzes its 
   compositional elements and imagery in relation to broader Aegean visual      
   traditions and to the controversy over potential forgeries. [1]              
 • The Ring of Nestor is used as a key visual example in discussions of a       
   Mycenaean ‘tree and pillar cult,’ where its engraved scene is compared with  
   other Mycenaean gold rings in museum collections to explore ritual and       
   religious imagery involving trees, pillars, and associated figures. [7]      
 • The Ring of Nestor and related Mycenaean gold rings are cited in studies of  
   Mycenaean plumes, where details such as apparent breasts or feather-like     
   adornments on sphinxes or similar figures are reinterpreted as possible plume
   or costume elements rather than purely anatomical features. [8]              
 • Evans’s work on the Ring of Nestor is treated as a classic reference on      
   Mycenaean goldwork and is cited in a technical archaeometallurgical study of 
   gold artifacts from four tholos tombs to contextualize broader conclusions   
   about Mycenaean gold production. [9]                                         

